MID_SE_Dan_Sci_C: <EDH>
MID_SE_Dan_Sci_C_001: My breath is the wind that blows sand forth.\nMy heart, a desolate dune...
MID_SE_Dan_Sci_C_002: Not bad, Sirocco! Is that one of yours?
MID_SE_Dan_Sci_C_003: The music of the words is lilting and pleasant,\nand the imagery conveys the narrator's feelings well.
MID_SE_Dan_Sci_C_004: Well, if it isn't Miss Dante! You honor me with your\ncompliments.
MID_SE_Dan_Sci_C_005: I'd be more than happy to treat you to further\nrecitations. Tonight, let's say—in bed?
MID_SE_Dan_Sci_C_006: Urgh! No. My interest in you begins and ends at your\npoetic ability.
MID_SE_Dan_Sci_C_007: Your coldness, your disdain... They wound me.
MID_SE_Dan_Sci_C_008: Yes, I'm sure you're devastated. It's not as if you have\nyour pick of partners.
MID_SE_Dan_Sci_C_009: In fact, I hear you've melted many a woman's heart\nwith your verse.
MID_SE_Dan_Sci_C_010: While your reasons for writing may be crude and\ntransactional, I can at least appreciate your talent.
MID_SE_Dan_Sci_C_011: As efficacious as my verse may be at wooing lovelies,\nthat in itself does not satisfy me.
MID_SE_Dan_Sci_C_012: Always at the back of my mind is the poet I don't\nhold a candle to.
MID_SE_Dan_Sci_C_013: Does such a brilliant poet exist?
MID_SE_Dan_Sci_C_014: Yes. And until I weave a poem that surpasses his,\nI will feel forever less than.
MID_SE_Dan_Sci_C_015: There was a poem of his I found inscribed in stone—\nwithin just a few lines, it had shattered my heart.
MID_SE_Dan_Sci_C_016: Doubtless, even the coldest of women would be\ninstantly charmed to hear it whispered in her ear.
MID_SE_Dan_Sci_C_017: My curiosity's growing unbearable. Who is this poet?
MID_SE_Dan_Sci_C_018: I've never met him, so I know him by neither face\nnor name.
MID_SE_Dan_Sci_C_019: A face hidden in the mists of existence... Such an\nenticing lacuna!
MID_SE_Dan_Sci_C_020: Yes... What I wouldn't give to speak to a poet\npossessed of such passion.
MID_SE_Dan_Sci_C_021: Though naturally, in terms of attractiveness, I'm\nalmost assuredly superior.
MID_SE_Dan_Sci_C_022: So you'd be better off spending an evening with me\nrather than him. Wouldn't you agree?
MID_SE_Dan_Sci_C_023: I would not. I'd rather spend an evening with him,\nengaged in a passionate exchange of ideas.
MID_SE_Dan_Sci_C_024: Ah, too bad. I suppose I'd better get to work on\nhoning my verse.
MID_SE_Dan_Sci_B: <EDH>
MID_SE_Dan_Sci_B_001: It's on the tip of my tongue... Gah! Perhaps I'm\ngetting tired.
MID_SE_Dan_Sci_B_002: Absorbed in your poetry? You keep to a routine.\nI'll give you that.
MID_SE_Dan_Sci_B_003: Here—some fruit. I find it an effective cure for\nwriter's block.
MID_SE_Dan_Sci_B_004: A welcome treat.
MID_SE_Dan_Sci_B_005: How to repay you? Perhaps I ought to take you by\nthe hand, lead you somewhere quiet...
MID_SE_Dan_Sci_B_006: Don't start! That's not why I'm here.
MID_SE_Dan_Sci_B_007: I was hoping you'd tell me more about that legendary\npoet you were recalling.
MID_SE_Dan_Sci_B_008: The brutality of darkening my doorstep, only to ask\nabout another man. I told you I never met him.
MID_SE_Dan_Sci_B_009: Yes, but you could at least give some more detail.\nI believe I may have figured out who it is.
MID_SE_Dan_Sci_B_010: It's someone you know, is it? And who is this\nfortunate fellow?
MID_SE_Dan_Sci_B_011: My father, I would guess. There's no better poet in\nhistory than Aeneus—none of his sex, at least.
MID_SE_Dan_Sci_B_012: Hm. I'm afraid you're wide of the mark.
MID_SE_Dan_Sci_B_013: The poet was recognized by Kalla eight years ago,\nand I'd heard he was 15 at the time.
MID_SE_Dan_Sci_B_014: Surely your father is older than that.
MID_SE_Dan_Sci_B_015: Hang on. A 15-year-old wrote it? And Kalla told\nyou this...eight years ago?
MID_SE_Dan_Sci_B_016: Sirocco, I know who it is. The answer—and I mean\nthis truly—will bring no happiness to either of us.
MID_SE_Dan_Sci_B_017: The legendary poet was me.
MID_SE_Dan_Sci_B_018: What? No. The poet was a man. He— Hm.
MID_SE_Dan_Sci_B_019: I suppose the poem never stated he was a man. I just\nassumed that by how it was written.
MID_SE_Dan_Sci_B_020: Do you recall any of the poem? You could give me a\nline or two.
MID_SE_Dan_Sci_B_021: Of course! I know it word by word. Allow me to\nrecite it...
MID_SE_Dan_Sci_B_022: Yes, there's no doubt in my mind. I wrote that poem\neight years ago.
MID_SE_Dan_Sci_B_023: H-how is that possible?
MID_SE_Dan_Sci_B_024: Whatever your sorrow, mine's the greater. I hoped,\nif not my father's, the work would be someone else's.
MID_SE_Dan_Sci_B_025: To think, the mysterious poet I longed to discover\nwas me all along! I'd fallen for my own reflection.
MID_SE_Dan_Sci_B_026: Count yourself lucky. The "man" you wanted to write\na poem to surpass is right here.
MID_SE_Dan_Sci_B_027: Hmm.
MID_SE_Dan_Sci_B_028: Why are you staring at me so intensely? You can't\nreally be that starstruck.
MID_SE_Dan_Sci_B_029: Never, staring at a beautiful woman, have I felt such\nsheer competitiveness.
MID_SE_Dan_Sci_B_030: Defeating you is now my most fervently held goal.\nI will surpass you, Darantea Aeneus!
MID_SE_Dan_Sci_B_031: Ohoho! You've laid down the gauntlet! I certainly\nprefer that to your flirtations.
MID_SE_Dan_Sci_B_032: Keep at it, spend a little less time in women's beds,\nand you may begin to approach my mastery.